2452	2475	2447	2450	the plain- speaking lawyer	526	“ Until this hint was given me by Beauchamp , I had thought East Lynne was settled upon your lordship ’s daughter . ”
2476	2484	2486	2487	the earl	445	“ There ’s nothing settled on her , ”
2499	2607	2486	2487	the earl	445	“ That comes of your thoughtless runaway marriages . I fell in love with General Conway ’s daughter , and she ran away with me , like a fool ; that is , we were both fools together for our pains . The general objected to me and said I must sow my wild oats before he would give me Mary ; so I took her to Gretna Green , and she became Countess of Mount Severn , without a settlement . It was an unfortunate affair , taking one thing with another . When her elopement was made known to the general , it killed him . ”
2608	2612	2614	2615	Mr. Carlyle	123	“ Killed him ! ”
2617	2724	2486	2487	the earl	445	“ It did . He had disease of the heart , and the excitement brought on the crisis . My poor wife never was happy from that hour ; she blamed herself for her father ’s death , and I believe it led to her own . She was ill for years ; the doctors called it consumption ; but it was more like a wasting insensibly away , and consumption never had been in her family . No luck ever attends runaway marriages ; I have noticed it since , in many , many instances ; something bad is sure to turn up from it . ”
2725	2737	2739	2740	Mr. Carlyle	123	“ There might have been a settlement executed after the marriage , ”
2754	2830	2486	2487	the earl	445	“ I know there might ; but there was not . My wife had possessed no fortune ; I was already deep in my career of extravagance , and neither of us thought of making provision for our future children ; or , if we thought of it , we did not do it . There is an old saying , Mr. Carlyle , that what may be done at any time is never done . ”
2835	2842	2844	2845	the earl	445	“ So my child is portionless , ”
2852	2978	2844	2845	the earl	445	“ The thought that it may be an embarrassing thing for her , were I to die before she is settled in life , crosses my mind when I am in a serious mood . That she will marry well , there is little doubt , for she possesses beauty in a rare degree , and has been reared as an English girl should be , not to frivolity and foppery . She was trained by her mother , who save for the mad act she was persuaded into by me , was all goodness and refinement , for the first twelve years of her life , and since then by an admirable governess . No fear that she will be decamping to Gretna Green . ”
2979	2987	2989	2990	the lawyer	530	“ She was a very lovely child , ”
2992	2997	2989	2990	the lawyer	530	“ I remember that . ”
2998	3128	2844	2845	the earl	445	“ Ay ; you have seen her at East Lynne , in her mother ’s lifetime . But , to return to business . If you become the purchaser of the East Lynne estate , Mr. Carlyle , it must be under the rose . The money that it brings , after paying off the mortgage , I must have , as I tell you , for my private use ; and you know I should not be able to touch a farthing of it if the confounded public got an inkling of the transfer . In the eyes of the world , the proprietor of East Lynne must be Lord Mount Severn -- at least for some little time afterwards . Perhaps you will not object to that . ”
